1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,030
Sweetheart, for three years,

2
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:05,004
my neighbor Walter Briggs called the city

3
00:00:05,004 --> 00:00:06,745
on me every single week.

4
00:00:06,925 --> 00:00:08,864
My grass was too long.

5
00:00:09,044 --> 00:00:11,435
My Christmas lights were too bright.

6
00:00:11,615 --> 00:00:14,035
He even called the police once because

7
00:00:14,035 --> 00:00:16,709
my grandchildren were laughing too loud in

8
00:00:16,709 --> 00:00:17,537
the backyard.

9
00:00:17,717 --> 00:00:19,939
And then, on Christmas Eve of two

10
00:00:19,939 --> 00:00:22,834
thousand fourteen, his house caught fire at

11
00:00:22,834 --> 00:00:23,440
midnight.

12
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:25,315
And I was the only one on

13
00:00:25,315 --> 00:00:27,552
the street who opened her door to

14
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:27,823
him.

15
00:00:28,173 --> 00:00:30,305
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

16
00:00:30,305 --> 00:00:32,060
that man did to me for three

17
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,130
years, what I found out about him

18
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:36,450
that Christmas, and the thing he left

19
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:38,206
on my porch in a shoebox the

20
00:00:38,206 --> 00:00:39,272
following spring.

21
00:00:39,452 --> 00:00:40,444
Stay with me,

22
00:00:40,444 --> 00:00:43,422
honey, because I've never been so wrong

23
00:00:43,422 --> 00:00:45,408
about a person in my life.

24
00:00:45,588 --> 00:00:48,084
And I've been alive a long time.

25
00:00:48,434 --> 00:00:51,260
Walter Briggs moved into the yellow house

26
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:53,535
next door in the spring of twenty

27
00:00:53,535 --> 00:00:54,018
eleven.

28
00:00:54,198 --> 00:00:55,450
A big, gray,

29
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:58,891
scowling man, maybe seventy-five,

30
00:00:58,891 --> 00:01:00,560
who lived alone.

31
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,415
When I took him a plate of

32
00:01:02,415 --> 00:01:04,348
cinnamon rolls to welcome him,

33
00:01:04,348 --> 00:01:06,474
he opened the door two inches and

34
00:01:06,474 --> 00:01:08,020
said, I don't eat sugar.

35
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,117
And shut it.

36
00:01:09,297 --> 00:01:11,489
Harold had passed by then,

37
00:01:11,489 --> 00:01:12,923
and I was lonely,

38
00:01:12,923 --> 00:01:16,043
honey, so I'd been looking forward to

39
00:01:16,043 --> 00:01:16,971
a neighbor.

40
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:19,910
The first complaint came a month later.

41
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:21,819
A letter from the city.

42
00:01:21,999 --> 00:01:24,321
Grass exceeding six inches.

43
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:25,604
Then another.

44
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:27,804
Leaves on the sidewalk.

45
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:29,762
Then my Christmas lights,

46
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:31,966
which I'd hung on the porch the

47
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:33,743
same way for forty years.

48
00:01:33,923 --> 00:01:37,255
Excessive illumination, the letter said.

49
00:01:37,435 --> 00:01:39,432
Excessive illumination!

50
00:01:39,612 --> 00:01:42,431
I'd had those same old colored bulbs

51
00:01:42,431 --> 00:01:44,233
since nineteen seventy.

52
00:01:44,413 --> 00:01:46,315
In a minute I'll tell you why

53
00:01:46,315 --> 00:01:48,546
those lights bothered him so much.

54
00:01:48,726 --> 00:01:50,607
It wasn't what I thought.

55
00:01:50,957 --> 00:01:53,258
It went on for three years,

56
00:01:53,258 --> 00:01:54,196
sweetheart.

57
00:01:54,376 --> 00:01:55,936
The bird feeder,

58
00:01:55,936 --> 00:01:56,814
the rats.

59
00:01:56,994 --> 00:01:59,637
My car parked too close to his

60
00:01:59,637 --> 00:02:00,430
driveway.

61
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:02,201
My wind chimes.

62
00:02:02,381 --> 00:02:04,900
My trash cans out too early.

63
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:07,027
Every week, a new letter,

64
00:02:07,027 --> 00:02:09,129
or a man from the city with

65
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:11,698
a clipboard, looking embarrassed.

66
00:02:11,878 --> 00:02:13,391
And the worst one,

67
00:02:13,391 --> 00:02:14,316
the police.

68
00:02:14,496 --> 00:02:16,464
Two officers at my door on a

69
00:02:16,464 --> 00:02:20,120
summer evening because my grandchildren were playing

70
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,228
in the sprinkler and laughing.

71
00:02:22,408 --> 00:02:24,115
Laughing, honey.

72
00:02:24,464 --> 00:02:26,229
The other neighbors hated him.

73
00:02:26,409 --> 00:02:27,442
Mrs.

74
00:02:27,622 --> 00:02:30,806
Patterson across the street called him the

75
00:02:30,806 --> 00:02:31,489
old crab.

76
00:02:31,669 --> 00:02:34,102
The young couple on the corner said

77
00:02:34,102 --> 00:02:36,606
he'd called the city about their dog

78
00:02:36,606 --> 00:02:38,135
barking, eleven times.

79
00:02:38,315 --> 00:02:40,806
People crossed the street when they saw

80
00:02:40,806 --> 00:02:42,402
him coming with his cane.

81
00:02:42,582 --> 00:02:44,335
At the block party,

82
00:02:44,335 --> 00:02:46,088
nobody invited him.

83
00:02:46,268 --> 00:02:47,643
And to be honest,

84
00:02:47,643 --> 00:02:50,959
honey, I started crossing the street too.

85
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:53,671
But there was one thing that bothered

86
00:02:53,671 --> 00:02:53,863
me.

87
00:02:54,043 --> 00:02:56,140
Every evening at six o'clock,

88
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:58,527
Walter Briggs sat on his porch in

89
00:02:58,527 --> 00:02:59,611
the same chair,

90
00:02:59,611 --> 00:03:01,130
with a cup of coffee,

91
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:03,806
and looked down the street toward the

92
00:03:03,806 --> 00:03:04,457
bus stop.

93
00:03:04,637 --> 00:03:07,278
Every evening, rain or snow,

94
00:03:07,278 --> 00:03:08,410
for an hour.

95
00:03:08,590 --> 00:03:10,529
Never waved at anybody.

96
00:03:10,709 --> 00:03:12,265
Never read a paper.

97
00:03:12,445 --> 00:03:13,746
Just sat there,

98
00:03:13,746 --> 00:03:15,916
looking at that bus stop.

99
00:03:16,096 --> 00:03:18,801
I wondered about it for three years.

100
00:03:19,151 --> 00:03:22,146
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

101
00:03:22,146 --> 00:03:24,456
tap the like button for me,

102
00:03:24,456 --> 00:03:25,398
sweetheart.

103
00:03:25,578 --> 00:03:28,945
It helps more people find these stories.

104
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:30,740
My daughter said,

105
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:32,735
Mom, just ignore him.

106
00:03:32,915 --> 00:03:34,784
Some people are just mean.

107
00:03:34,964 --> 00:03:36,652
And my son said,

108
00:03:36,652 --> 00:03:38,656
Mom, call a lawyer.

109
00:03:38,836 --> 00:03:40,299
That's harassment.

110
00:03:40,479 --> 00:03:43,011
But I'd been raised by Dorothy,

111
00:03:43,011 --> 00:03:45,707
honey, and my mother always said,

112
00:03:45,707 --> 00:03:47,341
nobody is born mean.

113
00:03:47,521 --> 00:03:49,611
Something makes them that way.

114
00:03:49,791 --> 00:03:51,857
Find out what it was.

115
00:03:52,207 --> 00:03:53,636
So that November,

116
00:03:53,636 --> 00:03:56,492
sweetheart, I tried one more time.

117
00:03:56,672 --> 00:03:58,847
I baked a loaf of my mother's

118
00:03:58,847 --> 00:04:01,023
bread, no sugar in it at all,

119
00:04:01,023 --> 00:04:03,498
and I walked it over and knocked.

120
00:04:03,678 --> 00:04:05,873
He opened the door two inches.

121
00:04:06,053 --> 00:04:07,614
I said, Walter,

122
00:04:07,614 --> 00:04:09,385
it's plain bread.

123
00:04:09,565 --> 00:04:10,911
No sugar.

124
00:04:11,091 --> 00:04:12,864
I just thought you might like a

125
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:13,379
neighbor.

126
00:04:13,559 --> 00:04:15,569
He looked at the bread for a

127
00:04:15,569 --> 00:04:16,287
long time.

128
00:04:16,467 --> 00:04:17,612
Then he said,

129
00:04:17,612 --> 00:04:19,725
I don't need a neighbor,

130
00:04:19,725 --> 00:04:20,078
Mrs.

131
00:04:20,258 --> 00:04:21,302
Miller.

132
00:04:21,482 --> 00:04:22,852
And he shut the door.

133
00:04:23,202 --> 00:04:24,493
On Christmas Eve,

134
00:04:24,493 --> 00:04:26,163
two thousand fourteen,

135
00:04:26,163 --> 00:04:28,516
I woke up at midnight to sirens

136
00:04:28,516 --> 00:04:31,248
and an orange light flickering on my

137
00:04:31,248 --> 00:04:32,235
bedroom wall.

138
00:04:32,415 --> 00:04:34,337
I looked out the window,

139
00:04:34,337 --> 00:04:37,460
and Walter Briggs's yellow house was on

140
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:37,860
fire.

141
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:40,687
Smoke pouring out of the kitchen windows.

142
00:04:40,867 --> 00:04:42,533
And there was Walter,

143
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:45,151
standing in his front yard in the

144
00:04:45,151 --> 00:04:47,928
snow, in his pajamas and bare feet,

145
00:04:47,928 --> 00:04:51,022
holding his cane and a little cardboard

146
00:04:51,022 --> 00:04:51,339
box.

147
00:04:51,689 --> 00:04:53,512
The fire trucks came.

148
00:04:53,692 --> 00:04:55,807
The whole street came out on their

149
00:04:55,807 --> 00:04:58,232
porches in their bathrobes and watched.

150
00:04:58,412 --> 00:05:00,520
And nobody went to him,

151
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:01,070
honey.

152
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:02,911
Not one person.

153
00:05:03,091 --> 00:05:04,112
Mrs.

154
00:05:04,292 --> 00:05:07,141
Patterson said, loud enough to hear,

155
00:05:07,141 --> 00:05:09,435
well, I guess he can call the

156
00:05:09,435 --> 00:05:10,701
city about that.

157
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:12,054
Somebody laughed.

158
00:05:12,234 --> 00:05:14,868
And Walter Briggs just stood there in

159
00:05:14,868 --> 00:05:16,719
the snow in his bare feet,

160
00:05:16,719 --> 00:05:18,427
seventy-eight years old,

161
00:05:18,427 --> 00:05:21,417
holding that little box against his chest.

162
00:05:21,767 --> 00:05:23,550
I put on my boots and my

163
00:05:23,550 --> 00:05:26,297
coat, and I grabbed Harold's old wool

164
00:05:26,297 --> 00:05:28,896
overcoat and a pair of his slippers

165
00:05:28,896 --> 00:05:31,346
that I'd never been able to throw

166
00:05:31,346 --> 00:05:31,717
away.

167
00:05:31,897 --> 00:05:34,190
And I walked across my lawn and

168
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:37,592
put that coat around Walter Briggs's shoulders

169
00:05:37,592 --> 00:05:39,885
and those slippers at his feet.

170
00:05:40,065 --> 00:05:42,271
He looked at me like he didn't

171
00:05:42,271 --> 00:05:43,374
know who I was.

172
00:05:43,554 --> 00:05:44,468
And I said,

173
00:05:44,468 --> 00:05:47,292
Walter, you're coming to my house.

174
00:05:47,472 --> 00:05:48,842
And he didn't argue.

175
00:05:49,192 --> 00:05:51,844
The fire department saved most of the

176
00:05:51,844 --> 00:05:54,137
house, but the kitchen was gone,

177
00:05:54,137 --> 00:05:56,286
and the smoke ruined the rest.

178
00:05:56,466 --> 00:05:59,476
So Walter Briggs spent Christmas in my

179
00:05:59,476 --> 00:06:00,901
spare room, honey.

180
00:06:01,081 --> 00:06:03,241
The man who'd called the police on

181
00:06:03,241 --> 00:06:04,320
my grandchildren.

182
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:05,902
That first night,

183
00:06:05,902 --> 00:06:07,469
I made him hot tea,

184
00:06:07,469 --> 00:06:10,025
and he sat at my kitchen table,

185
00:06:10,025 --> 00:06:13,489
still clutching that little cardboard box,

186
00:06:13,489 --> 00:06:14,148
shaking.

187
00:06:14,328 --> 00:06:16,453
I asked him what was in it.

188
00:06:16,802 --> 00:06:17,987
He opened it.

189
00:06:18,167 --> 00:06:19,860
Inside were letters,

190
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:20,791
sweetheart.

191
00:06:20,971 --> 00:06:22,467
Dozens of them,

192
00:06:22,467 --> 00:06:24,163
tied with string.

193
00:06:24,343 --> 00:06:27,133
And photographs of a little boy,

194
00:06:27,133 --> 00:06:29,139
and then a teenage boy,

195
00:06:29,139 --> 00:06:31,319
and then a young man in a

196
00:06:31,319 --> 00:06:32,888
soldier's uniform.

197
00:06:33,068 --> 00:06:33,949
And on top,

198
00:06:33,949 --> 00:06:35,789
a folded American flag,

199
00:06:35,789 --> 00:06:38,831
the triangle kind they give the family

200
00:06:38,831 --> 00:06:40,592
at a military funeral.

201
00:06:40,772 --> 00:06:43,602
Walter said, this is my son,

202
00:06:43,602 --> 00:06:44,208
Danny.

203
00:06:44,388 --> 00:06:46,762
He took the bus to the recruiting

204
00:06:46,762 --> 00:06:49,423
office from that bus stop in nineteen

205
00:06:49,423 --> 00:06:50,286
sixty-seven.

206
00:06:50,636 --> 00:06:53,487
Danny Briggs was killed in Vietnam in

207
00:06:53,487 --> 00:06:55,105
nineteen sixty-eight.

208
00:06:55,285 --> 00:06:56,737
He was nineteen.

209
00:06:56,917 --> 00:06:59,352
Walter's wife died of grief,

210
00:06:59,352 --> 00:07:01,700
he said, a few years later.

211
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:04,034
And Walter had sat on a porch

212
00:07:04,034 --> 00:07:07,153
every evening at six o'clock for forty-six

213
00:07:07,153 --> 00:07:09,604
years, at three different houses,

214
00:07:09,604 --> 00:07:11,238
looking at a bus stop.

215
00:07:11,418 --> 00:07:14,333
Because that's the time Danny's bus used

216
00:07:14,333 --> 00:07:16,154
to come home from school.

217
00:07:16,334 --> 00:07:19,137
And he still couldn't stop watching for

218
00:07:19,137 --> 00:07:19,353
it.

219
00:07:19,702 --> 00:07:21,846
I asked him why he'd moved to

220
00:07:21,846 --> 00:07:23,177
our street, honey,

221
00:07:23,177 --> 00:07:25,542
of all the streets in Mansfield.

222
00:07:25,722 --> 00:07:28,244
He said, because you can see the

223
00:07:28,244 --> 00:07:30,529
old bus stop from this porch.

224
00:07:30,709 --> 00:07:32,265
The one Danny took.

225
00:07:32,445 --> 00:07:34,307
When they built the new road,

226
00:07:34,307 --> 00:07:35,591
they moved the stop.

227
00:07:35,771 --> 00:07:38,108
But the old bench is still there,

228
00:07:38,108 --> 00:07:39,312
by the drugstore.

229
00:07:39,492 --> 00:07:42,166
I'd been living three blocks away for

230
00:07:42,166 --> 00:07:43,033
forty years.

231
00:07:43,213 --> 00:07:45,500
When the yellow house came up for

232
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,648
sale, I bought it the same day.

233
00:07:47,998 --> 00:07:50,224
So far, the complaints,

234
00:07:50,224 --> 00:07:52,258
the police, the fire,

235
00:07:52,258 --> 00:07:53,419
and the box.

236
00:07:53,599 --> 00:07:55,778
And then he told me about the

237
00:07:55,778 --> 00:07:56,304
lights.

238
00:07:56,484 --> 00:07:57,727
He said, Ruth,

239
00:07:57,727 --> 00:08:00,746
the last Christmas Danny was home,

240
00:08:00,746 --> 00:08:02,699
before he shipped out,

241
00:08:02,699 --> 00:08:05,895
we hung those same old colored bulbs

242
00:08:05,895 --> 00:08:07,050
on our porch.

243
00:08:07,230 --> 00:08:09,459
Exactly like yours.

244
00:08:09,639 --> 00:08:11,434
When I see your lights,

245
00:08:11,434 --> 00:08:12,762
I see that porch.

246
00:08:12,942 --> 00:08:14,405
I can't breathe.

247
00:08:14,585 --> 00:08:16,853
And I didn't know how to tell

248
00:08:16,853 --> 00:08:17,870
anybody that.

249
00:08:18,050 --> 00:08:19,478
So I called the city.

250
00:08:19,829 --> 00:08:21,923
And the children laughing,

251
00:08:21,923 --> 00:08:22,406
honey.

252
00:08:22,586 --> 00:08:25,030
He said, Danny used to play in

253
00:08:25,030 --> 00:08:27,636
the sprinkler exactly like that.

254
00:08:27,816 --> 00:08:30,403
I'd hear your grandchildren and I'd run

255
00:08:30,403 --> 00:08:32,658
to the window thinking it was him.

256
00:08:32,838 --> 00:08:34,022
Every time.

257
00:08:34,202 --> 00:08:36,365
And then I'd see it wasn't,

258
00:08:36,365 --> 00:08:39,008
and it was like losing him again.

259
00:08:39,188 --> 00:08:40,999
I'm not a mean man,

260
00:08:40,999 --> 00:08:41,476
Ruth.

261
00:08:41,656 --> 00:08:43,727
I'm a man who can't stand the

262
00:08:43,727 --> 00:08:45,440
sound of a happy family.

263
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:46,932
There's a difference.

264
00:08:47,283 --> 00:08:49,633
We sat at my kitchen table until

265
00:08:49,633 --> 00:08:51,103
four in the morning,

266
00:08:51,103 --> 00:08:51,543
honey.

267
00:08:51,723 --> 00:08:54,429
He told me everything about Danny.

268
00:08:54,609 --> 00:08:57,999
That he loved baseball and hated peas.

269
00:08:58,179 --> 00:09:00,686
That he wanted to be a veterinarian.

270
00:09:00,866 --> 00:09:03,637
That he'd written his father a letter

271
00:09:03,637 --> 00:09:05,434
every week from Vietnam,

272
00:09:05,434 --> 00:09:07,456
and every single one ended,

273
00:09:07,456 --> 00:09:09,553
save me a seat on the porch,

274
00:09:09,553 --> 00:09:09,853
Dad.

275
00:09:10,033 --> 00:09:12,483
Walter had saved him a seat for

276
00:09:12,483 --> 00:09:13,748
forty-six years.

277
00:09:14,098 --> 00:09:15,660
On Christmas morning,

278
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,265
my children and grandchildren came,

279
00:09:18,265 --> 00:09:21,167
and found Walter Briggs at my breakfast

280
00:09:21,167 --> 00:09:23,177
table in Harold's bathrobe.

281
00:09:23,357 --> 00:09:25,992
My son nearly dropped the presents.

282
00:09:26,172 --> 00:09:28,536
I pulled him into the kitchen and

283
00:09:28,536 --> 00:09:29,968
told him everything.

284
00:09:30,148 --> 00:09:31,076
And my son,

285
00:09:31,076 --> 00:09:33,857
who'd wanted me to call a lawyer,

286
00:09:33,857 --> 00:09:36,301
went out and sat down next to

287
00:09:36,301 --> 00:09:37,396
that old man,

288
00:09:37,396 --> 00:09:39,587
and asked him about Danny.

289
00:09:39,767 --> 00:09:42,693
And then he asked him about Vietnam.

290
00:09:42,873 --> 00:09:45,286
My son served in the Gulf War,

291
00:09:45,286 --> 00:09:46,170
sweetheart.

292
00:09:46,521 --> 00:09:48,713
And then my youngest grandson,

293
00:09:48,713 --> 00:09:50,394
Charlie, who was eight,

294
00:09:50,394 --> 00:09:52,659
climbed up on the chair next to

295
00:09:52,659 --> 00:09:55,509
Walter, and showed him his new baseball

296
00:09:55,509 --> 00:09:55,948
glove.

297
00:09:56,128 --> 00:09:57,460
And Walter Briggs,

298
00:09:57,460 --> 00:09:59,533
the old crab of Maple Drive,

299
00:09:59,533 --> 00:10:01,902
took that glove in his hands and

300
00:10:01,902 --> 00:10:04,789
turned it over and smelled the leather.

301
00:10:04,969 --> 00:10:05,811
And he said,

302
00:10:05,811 --> 00:10:08,196
Charlie, would you like me to show

303
00:10:08,196 --> 00:10:09,950
you how to break this in?

304
00:10:10,130 --> 00:10:12,173
It's how I taught my boy.

305
00:10:12,523 --> 00:10:14,369
And for those three months,

306
00:10:14,369 --> 00:10:17,584
honey, I learned something about Walter Briggs.

307
00:10:17,764 --> 00:10:20,258
He fixed everything in my house that

308
00:10:20,258 --> 00:10:21,851
Harold had left broken.

309
00:10:22,031 --> 00:10:24,167
The drip in the bathroom sink.

310
00:10:24,347 --> 00:10:26,159
The squeak in the back door.

311
00:10:26,339 --> 00:10:28,440
The loose step on the porch.

312
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:32,039
He made breakfast every morning at six,

313
00:10:32,039 --> 00:10:33,880
eggs and plain toast,

314
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:34,669
no sugar.

315
00:10:34,849 --> 00:10:36,336
And he started,

316
00:10:36,336 --> 00:10:38,518
very slowly, to smile.

317
00:10:38,698 --> 00:10:41,303
The first time he laughed out loud,

318
00:10:41,303 --> 00:10:43,239
at something Charlie said,

319
00:10:43,239 --> 00:10:45,547
he looked surprised at himself.

320
00:10:45,898 --> 00:10:48,222
Walter lived in my spare room for

321
00:10:48,222 --> 00:10:51,180
three months while his house was repaired.

322
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:53,766
He and Charlie broke in that glove

323
00:10:53,766 --> 00:10:55,888
in my backyard every Saturday.

324
00:10:56,068 --> 00:10:57,333
And in April,

325
00:10:57,333 --> 00:10:59,667
he moved back next door.

326
00:10:59,847 --> 00:11:01,178
The next morning,

327
00:11:01,178 --> 00:11:03,527
I found a shoebox on my porch.

328
00:11:03,707 --> 00:11:06,276
Inside was a brand new string of

329
00:11:06,276 --> 00:11:09,326
old-fashioned colored Christmas bulbs,

330
00:11:09,326 --> 00:11:10,369
the big kind,

331
00:11:10,369 --> 00:11:12,055
still in the package.

332
00:11:12,235 --> 00:11:13,361
And a note.

333
00:11:13,712 --> 00:11:14,999
The note said,

334
00:11:14,999 --> 00:11:17,299
Ruth, for next Christmas.

335
00:11:17,479 --> 00:11:19,128
Please hang them bright.

336
00:11:19,308 --> 00:11:21,572
Danny would want me to see them.

337
00:11:21,752 --> 00:11:23,400
And thank you for the coat.

338
00:11:23,580 --> 00:11:24,614
Walter.

339
00:11:24,794 --> 00:11:26,658
And that next Christmas,

340
00:11:26,658 --> 00:11:29,532
honey, we hung those lights together,

341
00:11:29,532 --> 00:11:31,318
on both of our porches.

342
00:11:31,498 --> 00:11:33,867
And the whole street came over.

343
00:11:34,047 --> 00:11:35,382
Even Mrs.

344
00:11:35,562 --> 00:11:36,816
Patterson.

345
00:11:36,996 --> 00:11:39,562
And Walter said hello to every one

346
00:11:39,562 --> 00:11:40,769
of them by name.

347
00:11:41,119 --> 00:11:43,654
Walter passed four years later,

348
00:11:43,654 --> 00:11:44,799
at eighty-two.

349
00:11:44,979 --> 00:11:45,816
At the end,

350
00:11:45,816 --> 00:11:48,402
he asked to be buried with Danny's

351
00:11:48,402 --> 00:11:51,217
letters, and he asked Charlie to have

352
00:11:51,217 --> 00:11:52,967
his old baseball glove.

353
00:11:53,147 --> 00:11:55,794
Charlie plays college baseball now.

354
00:11:55,974 --> 00:11:57,947
He wears it in every game.

355
00:11:58,127 --> 00:11:59,428
And on my porch,

356
00:11:59,428 --> 00:12:01,784
every evening at six o'clock,

357
00:12:01,784 --> 00:12:03,654
there's an empty chair.

358
00:12:03,834 --> 00:12:06,059
I've saved Walter a seat,

359
00:12:06,059 --> 00:12:07,038
sweetheart.

360
00:12:07,218 --> 00:12:09,169
And Danny, too.

361
00:12:09,519 --> 00:12:12,264
Here's what I want you to remember,

362
00:12:12,264 --> 00:12:12,735
honey.

363
00:12:12,915 --> 00:12:14,741
Behind every mean neighbor,

364
00:12:14,741 --> 00:12:16,839
there's a story you don't know.

365
00:12:17,019 --> 00:12:18,563
My mother was right.

366
00:12:18,743 --> 00:12:20,484
Nobody is born mean.

367
00:12:20,664 --> 00:12:22,396
Tell me in the comments,

368
00:12:22,396 --> 00:12:25,428
sweetheart, have you ever been wrong about

369
00:12:25,428 --> 00:12:26,005
someone?

370
00:12:26,185 --> 00:12:28,732
There are more of my family's stories

371
00:12:28,732 --> 00:12:31,141
in the playlist right there on your

372
00:12:31,141 --> 00:12:33,274
screen, and I'll see you in the

373
00:12:33,274 --> 00:12:33,894
next one.

374
00:12:34,074 --> 00:12:34,863
Good night.

375
00:12:35,043 --> 00:12:36,529
Merry Christmas.
